#: ../../../setuptools.rst:3
msgid "Building and Distributing Packages with Setuptools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:5
msgid ""
"``Setuptools`` is a collection of enhancements to the Python ``distutils`` "
"that allow developers to more easily build Python packages, including those "
"that have dependencies on other packages and C/C++ extension modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:9
msgid ""
"Packages built and distributed using ``setuptools`` look to the user like "
"ordinary Python packages based on the ``distutils``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:12
msgid "Feature Highlights:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:14
msgid ""
"Create `Python Eggs <http://peak.telecommunity.com/DevCenter/PythonEggs>`_ - "
"a single-file importable distribution format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:17
msgid "Enhanced support for accessing data files hosted in zipped packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:19
msgid ""
"Automatically include all packages in your source tree, without listing them "
"individually in setup.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:22
msgid ""
"Automatically include all relevant files in your source distributions, "
"without needing to create a :ref:`MANIFEST.in <Using MANIFEST.in>` file, and "
"without having to force regeneration of the ``MANIFEST`` file when your "
"source tree changes [#manifest]_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:27
msgid ""
"Automatically generate wrapper scripts or Windows (console and GUI) .exe "
"files for any number of \"main\" functions in your project.  (Note: this is "
"not a py2exe replacement; the .exe files rely on the local Python "
"installation.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:31
msgid ""
"Transparent Cython support, so that your setup.py can list ``.pyx`` files "
"and still work even when the end-user doesn't have Cython installed (as long "
"as you include the Cython-generated C in your source distribution)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:35
msgid ""
"Command aliases - create project-specific, per-user, or site-wide shortcut "
"names for commonly used commands and options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:38
msgid ""
"Deploy your project in \"development mode\", such that it's available on "
"``sys.path``, yet can still be edited directly from its source checkout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:41
msgid ""
"Easily extend the distutils with new commands or ``setup()`` arguments, and "
"distribute/reuse your extensions for multiple projects, without copying code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:44
msgid ""
"Create extensible applications and frameworks that automatically discover "
"extensions, using simple \"entry points\" declared in a project's setup "
"script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:47
msgid ""
"Full support for PEP 420 via ``find_namespace_packages()``, which is also "
"backwards compatible to the existing ``find_packages()`` for Python >= 3.3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:52
msgid "Developer's Guide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:54
msgid ""
"The developer's guide has been updated. See the :doc:`most recent version "
"<userguide/index>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:87
msgid "TRANSITIONAL NOTE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:89
msgid ""
"Setuptools automatically calls ``declare_namespace()`` for you at runtime, "
"but future versions may *not*.  This is because the automatic declaration "
"feature has some negative side effects, such as needing to import all "
"namespace packages during the initialization of the ``pkg_resources`` "
"runtime, and also the need for ``pkg_resources`` to be explicitly imported "
"before any namespace packages work at all.  In some future releases, you'll "
"be responsible for including your own declaration lines, and the automatic "
"declaration feature will be dropped to get rid of the negative side effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:98
msgid ""
"During the remainder of the current development cycle, therefore, setuptools "
"will warn you about missing ``declare_namespace()`` calls in your ``__init__."
"py`` files, and you should correct these as soon as possible before the "
"compatibility support is removed. Namespace packages without declaration "
"lines will not work correctly once a user has upgraded to a later version, "
"so it's important that you make this change now in order to avoid having "
"your code break in the field. Our apologies for the inconvenience, and thank "
"you for your patience."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:124
msgid "setup.cfg-only projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:128
msgid ""
"If ``setup.py`` is missing from the project directory when a :pep:`517` "
"build is invoked, ``setuptools`` emulates a dummy ``setup.py`` file "
"containing only a ``setuptools.setup()`` call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:134
msgid ""
":pep:`517` doesn't support editable installs so this is currently "
"incompatible with ``pip install -e .``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:137
msgid ""
"This means that you can have a Python project with all build configuration "
"specified in ``setup.cfg``, without a ``setup.py`` file, if you **can rely "
"on** your project always being built by a :pep:`517`/:pep:`518` compatible "
"frontend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:142
msgid "To use this feature:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:144
msgid ""
"Specify build requirements and :pep:`517` build backend in ``pyproject."
"toml``. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:148
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\n"
"  \"setuptools >= 40.9.0\",\n"
"]\n"
"build-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:156
msgid ""
"Use a :pep:`517` compatible build frontend, such as ``pip >= 19`` or "
"``build``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:160
msgid ""
"As :pep:`517` is new, support is not universal, and frontends that do "
"support it may still have bugs. For compatibility, you may want to put a "
"``setup.py`` file containing only a ``setuptools.setup()`` invocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:167
msgid "Configuration API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:169
msgid ""
"Some automation tools may wish to access data from a configuration file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:171
msgid ""
"``Setuptools`` exposes a ``read_configuration()`` function for parsing "
"``metadata`` and ``options`` sections into a dictionary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:175
msgid ""
"from setuptools.config import read_configuration\n"
"\n"
"conf_dict = read_configuration(\"/home/user/dev/package/setup.cfg\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:182
msgid ""
"By default, ``read_configuration()`` will read only the file provided in the "
"first argument. To include values from other configuration files which could "
"be in various places, set the ``find_others`` keyword argument to ``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:187
msgid ""
"If you have only a configuration file but not the whole package, you can "
"still try to get data out of it with the help of the "
"``ignore_option_errors`` keyword argument. When it is set to ``True``, all "
"options with errors possibly produced by directives, such as ``attr:`` and "
"others, will be silently ignored. As a consequence, the resulting dictionary "
"will include no such options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:204
msgid "Forum and Bug Tracker"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:206
msgid ""
"Please use `GitHub Discussions`_ for questions and discussion about "
"setuptools, and the `setuptools bug tracker`_ ONLY for issues you have "
"confirmed via the forum are actual bugs, and which you have reduced to a "
"minimal set of steps to reproduce."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../setuptools.rst:218
msgid ""
"The default behaviour for ``setuptools`` will work well for pure Python "
"packages, or packages with simple C extensions (that don't require any "
"special C header). See :ref:`Controlling files in the distribution` and :doc:"
"`userguide/datafiles` for more information about complex scenarios, if you "
"want to include other types of files."
msgstr ""
